<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gujarat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gujarat is a region in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> with a long coastline and a history of maritime trade. It was brought under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a> in the course of its western campaigns. In the eighteenth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a> extended its authority and revenue claims into Gujarat during its expansion across India.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gujarat.jpg" alt="Gujarat" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rani_ki_vav_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Gujarat lies along the western coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and has the longest coastline of any Indian state, most of it on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Kathiawar</a> peninsula. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a>, and on the northwest the Pakistani province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rann-of-kutch">Rann of Kutch</a>, a seasonally marshy saline clay desert, separates it. Historically the north was known as Anarta, the Kathiawar peninsula as Saurastra and the south as Lata. The name is generally believed to derive from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurjars">Gurjars</a>: parts of modern Rajasthan and Gujarat were known as the Gurjarat region for centuries before the Mughal period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Gujarat was one of the central areas of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a>, and about fifty settlements of that civilisation have been found there, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothal">Lothal</a>, believed to have been one of the first seaports, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dholavira">Dholavira</a>, one of the largest archaeological sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and Gola Dhoro. Coastal cities, chiefly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharuch">Bharuch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khambhat">Khambhat</a>, were ports and trading centres under the Maurya and Gupta empires and the Saka dynasties of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-satraps">Western Satraps</a>. In the early 8th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> tried to expand southeast from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>; campaigns fought in 730 ended in their defeat, probably by a coalition of Indian rulers.</p><p>Qutbuddin Aibak tried and failed to annex Gujarat in 1197. From 1297 to 1300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, destroyed the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patan-gujarat">Anhilwara</a> and brought it into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> sacked Delhi, the governor Zafar Khan Muzaffar asserted independence, and his son Sultan Ahmed Shah (ruled 1411 to 1442) made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmedabad">Ahmedabad</a> the capital. In 1664 and again in 1672 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a>. By then the Portuguese, followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English, had bases on the coast; after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-diu-1509">Battle of Diu</a> the Portuguese held enclaves including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daman">Daman</a> and Diu until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> took them on 19 December 1961.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Gujarat is a state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gandhinagar">Gandhinagar</a>, a planned city, and its largest city at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmedabad">Ahmedabad</a>. Gujarati is the official language, and the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 60,439,692 people. Its tradition of seafaring and long-distance trade carried Gujarati merchants to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Somnath, the Sun Temple at Modhera, the Jain pilgrimage mountain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatrunjaya">Shatrunjaya</a> at Palitana and the Gir forest, the only home of wild Asiatic lions, are among its lasting sites, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> was born at Porbandar.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gujarat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Grown on the coasts of northern and western Sumatra, it was shipped through Aceh to <b>Gujarat</b>, the Red Sea and the Ottoman lands by a route that bypassed the Portuguese, and later sold to Chinese, English, Dutch and French buyers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1461–1462 Mahmud Khalji of Malwa invaded and besieged Bidar but was driven back with help from <b>Gujarat</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In about 739 the Chalukya governor in <b>Gujarat</b>, Avanijanashraya Pulakeshin, repulsed an Arab raid from Sindh.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>He took the Ismaili-ruled city of Multan in 1006 and again in 1010, defeated Anandapala at Chach in 1008, plundered Nagarkot, Thanesar, Mathura and Kanauj, and in 1025–1026 marched across the Thar desert to sack the temple of Somnath on the <b>Gujarat</b> coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1391 Sultan Nasir-ud-Din Muhammad bin Tughluq appointed Zafar Khan governor of <b>Gujarat</b> with the title Muzaffar Khan, and in 1392 he defeated the former governor Farhat-ul-Mulk at the battle of Kamboi and occupied Anhilwada Patan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gujarat-sultanate">Gujarat Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>This gave the Guptas access to the prosperous port cities of <b>Gujarat</b> and the Arabian Sea trade routes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
